%erg 0.1
Title: erg init silently downgrades an asset when the running binary predates the stamp
Created: 2026-09-10
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-09-10T09:40Z HDMX-coding-agent created

2026-09-16T08:08Z HDMX-coding-agent amended Plan: a preserving run no longer rewrites .erg-assets. Stamping assets the run declined to touch records a state that never happened and erases the rollback evidence, so the NEXT run misreads the same situation as a local edit -- the defect this ticket removes. Outside planned scope, flagged for the verify panel.
2026-09-16T08:08Z HDMX-coding-agent note a line-oriented grep is a vacuous ratchet for the exit-2 wording: cmdInit's stale comment wrapped mid-phrase, so grep -n '2 local edits skipped' returned zero against the live violation. Normalise first (strip comment markers, join lines, squeeze spaces) and prove the pattern fires on a known-positive case before trusting it.
2026-09-16T08:09Z HDMX-coding-agent note the shape guard (looksLikeBuildDate) was not in the Plan and is load-bearing: lexical ordering only means something once both sides are known to be dates. tests/test_check.sh's own drift fixture stamps 'date: y', which sorts above every digit, so an unguarded compare read every malformed stamp as a rollback and would have frozen those stores' assets permanently. That fixture is the standing positive control.
--- body ---
## Context

`isCleanUpgrade` (`src/go/manifest.go`) decides overwrite-vs-preserve by asking
whether the on-disk asset matches the `.erg-assets` stamp. It never asks in
which *direction* the two binaries differ. So the "clean upgrade" branch fires
identically for an upgrade and for a rollback, and a binary older than the one
that wrote the stamp will happily overwrite a newer asset with its own older
embedded copy.

Reproduced on a fixture (2026-09-10), using aedist's committed binary
(`8331a94`, 2026-06-05) against the current one (`089cd31`, 2026-06-29):

```
1. state before                    AGENTS.md=1862  stamp=07c11767031b
2. refresh with the current binary AGENTS.md=2141  stamp=beda994b2c37
3. later, the repo's own binary:
     WARN AGENTS.md: embedded version differs from the .erg-assets stamp
          (binary upgraded since last init) -- run 'erg init' to refresh
     init: refreshed tickets/AGENTS.md
                                   AGENTS.md=1862  stamp=07c11767031b
```

Three separate defects compound at step 3:

1. **The warning asserts a direction it never checks.** `assetDriftSignal`
   reads "binary upgraded since last init". Here the binary is three months
   *older* than the stamp. `assetDriftWarnings` compares hashes for inequality
   and infers an ordering from nothing.
2. **The advice the warning gives is what performs the damage.** It says to run
   `erg init`; doing so reverts the asset.
3. **The revert reports itself as a refresh.** `init: refreshed
   tickets/AGENTS.md`, exit 0. Nothing in the output distinguishes a three-month
   rollback of a git-tracked file from a legitimate upgrade.

This is not hypothetical housekeeping. It is the failure mode an agent walks
into whenever it helps across repos: the obvious gesture — run the newest erg
you have against a stale adopter — leaves that repo primed to revert itself at
the next `erg init`, guided by a warning that will be printed at every session.
The trap is what makes `erg update && erg init` mandatory as a pair rather than
merely canonical, and nothing in the code enforces the pairing today.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/manifest.go` — `isCleanUpgrade` (the direction-blind decision),
  `assetDriftWarnings` and `assetDriftSignal` (the inverted message),
  `writeManifest` around line 57 where `buildDate` is already recorded
- `src/go/init.go` — `installAssets`, which acts on the verdict and prints
  `refreshed`
- `src/go/version.go` — `buildDate`, `vcsRevision`, both set via `-ldflags`
- `src/go/update.go` — the post-update hint that emits the same signal

## The material for the fix already exists

The stamp records `rev:` and `date:` (written from `buildDate`), and the
running binary knows its own `buildDate`. Both sides of the comparison are
therefore already on disk and in the binary; no new provenance needs
inventing. A stamp older than the running binary is an upgrade; a stamp newer
is a rollback, and a rollback must not be silently applied.

Note the one case that must keep working: a stamp with no date, or an
unparseable one, from an older erg. Absent provenance is not evidence of a
rollback, so it must degrade to today's behaviour, not to a refusal.

## Actions

1. Give `isCleanUpgrade` the stamp's date (or revision) and the running
   binary's `buildDate`. When the stamp is strictly newer, do not treat the
   match as a clean upgrade: preserve, and say why.
2. Fix `assetDriftSignal` to state the direction it has actually established —
   "binary upgraded since last init" only when that is true, and a distinct
   message naming the rollback when the binary is the older one.
3. Make the rollback message name the remedy (`erg update` first), since the
   user in this state is one command away from being correct.
4. Make `installAssets` print `downgraded` rather than `refreshed` if a
   deliberate rollback is ever performed (`--force`), so the log never claims a
   refresh it did not do.
5. Missing or unparseable stamp date: fall through to current behaviour.

## Test

Red step: a fixture whose `.erg-assets` stamp carries a date newer than the
running binary's `buildDate`, with the on-disk asset matching that stamp.
Assert `erg init` preserves and exits 2. Against today's code it overwrites and
exits 0 — run it and watch it fail before the fix, or it proves nothing.

The positive control is the arm that must not regress: a stamp *older* than the
running binary, on-disk asset matching it, must still upgrade cleanly and exit
0. That is the ordinary path and it is the one a direction check is most likely
to break.

Third arm: a stamp with no `date:` field must behave exactly as today.

Fourth arm, on the message rather than the exit code: assert the warning text
for a rollback does not contain "upgraded". Defect 1 above is invisible to any
assertion that only reads exit codes.

## Verification

- [ ] fixture with a newer stamp: asset preserved, exit 2, message names `erg update`
- [ ] fixture with an older stamp: asset upgraded, exit 0 (no regression)
- [ ] fixture with a dateless stamp: behaviour byte-identical to today
- [ ] the drift warning never claims "upgraded" when the binary is the older side
- [ ] the fixture reproduction in Context, replayed end to end, no longer reverts

## Invariants

- A genuine upgrade must still be silent and automatic. This ticket must not
  turn the ordinary `erg update && erg init` path into a prompt.
- A real local edit is still preserved, unchanged, by every path.
- No new provenance is written into `.erg-assets`; the fix uses fields the
  stamp already carries.
- Stores written by an erg predating the `date:` field keep working.

## Exit criteria

- A binary older than the stamp cannot overwrite the asset without `--force`
- No message asserts an ordering the code has not established
- The fixture in Context replays clean
- `erg update && erg init` is enforced as a pair by the code, not only
  documented as canonical in `erg init --help`

## Related

- 0277, 0278 — same investigation (the 11-repo asset sweep of 2026-09-10). This
  defect is distinct from both: 0278 is about a table that is empty, this is
  about a comparison that is direction-blind. Neither 0277 nor 0278 describes
  it, and it fires on repos that both of them consider healthy.

## Imagine

**Why now / why this scope.** The reproduction in Context is not a corner
case: any agent carrying a newer `erg` into an older adopter repo (the
ordinary cross-repo helping gesture the ticket names) hits this on the very
next `erg init`, guided there by a warning whose own words are backwards. The
blast radius is one file (`manifest.go`) plus the two call sites that already
consume its verdict (`init.go`, `update.go`), so this is sized for a single
PR, not a decomposition.

**Fix path confirmed, with one addition the ticket's Actions section
undercounts.** `writeManifest` (manifest.go:62-69) already writes `rev:` and
`date:` into `.erg-assets` on every init. But `parseManifest`
(manifest.go:107-126) only extracts lines matching the `" sha256:"` asset
pattern — the `rev:`/`date:` header lines are silently skipped today, so
nothing currently reads them back. "Both sides of the comparison are already
on disk and in the binary" (ticket, § The material for the fix already
exists) is true of what gets *written*, not of what gets *parsed* — the
manifest reader needs a new field (stamped date, and optionally rev) before
`isCleanUpgrade` can be handed it. This is a small addition (extend
`parseManifest`'s return type or add a sibling accessor), not a redesign, and
it does not contradict the ticket's premise — it refines Action 1 from "give
isCleanUpgrade the date" to "read the date, then give it to isCleanUpgrade."

**Simplest comparison: plain string compare, not date parsing.** `buildDate`
is stamped by the Makefile as `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`
(Makefile:35) — fixed-width ISO-8601 UTC — and the codebase already relies on
lexical string comparison of exactly this format to order two build dates:
`version.go:237` (`buildDate > otherDate`) inside `cmdVersion`'s
same-binary-family discovery. Reusing that precedent means no new
`time.Parse` path, no timezone/format-drift risk, and no new dependency —
`stampDate > buildDate` (or `<`, depending on which side is asked) is the
whole comparison. This is simpler than the ticket's own phrasing ("stamp's
date ... running binary's buildDate") might suggest if read as implying a
parsed-time comparison; recommend making the string-compare choice explicit
in the implementation to head off a future PR "upgrading" it to
`time.Parse` for no benefit.

**No monotonic build-number exists to use instead.** Checked `version.go` for
a simpler ordering signal than a date: the only two provenance fields the
binary carries are `buildDate` (string, lexically ordered by construction)
and `vcsRevision` (a short git hash — MinhHaDuong's commits are not
monotonic short hashes, so revision alone cannot express order without a
`git merge-base --is-ancestor` check, which is unavailable offline and far
heavier than string comparison). `buildDate` is therefore not just the
simplest available signal, it is the only one that orders without extra
machinery. Antipattern scan verdict: **no YAGNI/premature-abstraction risk
in the proposed fix** — it adds one struct field's worth of state
(stamp date) and one string comparison, reusing an existing format and an
existing comparison idiom rather than inventing either.

**`assetDriftSignal` split does not break `update.go`'s consumer.**
`update.go:228` greps the re-exec'd binary's `erg check` output for the
literal `assetDriftSignal` string, but that grep only fires in the
just-updated-so-strictly-newer context (`erg update && erg init`), which by
construction is always the upgrade direction. Splitting the constant into an
upgrade-direction message and a distinct rollback-direction message (ticket
Action 2) leaves `update.go`'s existing grep target (the upgrade message)
intact; the new rollback message needs no consumer of its own beyond
`assetDriftWarnings`'s own stderr output. No coordinated multi-file rename is
needed — just add the second string alongside the first.

**Premise check: LIVE.** Confirmed by direct reading, not inference:

- `isCleanUpgrade` (manifest.go:163-173) takes `(diskHash, stampedHash,
  known []string)` and returns `diskHash == stampedHash` (or a membership
  test against `known`). No date, no rev, no direction — only equality. The
  doc comment above it (manifest.go:158-162) confirms the design intent
  never considered direction ("a pristine prior release... as opposed to a
  local edit"), silently conflating "prior" with "any release."
- The one call site, `init.go:150` —
  `isCleanUpgrade(diskHash, stamps[name], knownAssetHashes(rel))` — passes no
  date or build-metadata argument at all, so the direction-blindness is not
  masked upstream either.
- `assetDriftWarnings` (manifest.go:184-208) computes drift as
  `stamp != sha256hex(embeddedContent)` (line 202) — inequality only — and
  unconditionally formats it via the fixed constant `assetDriftSignal =
  "...binary upgraded since last init"` (manifest.go:24) regardless of which
  side is actually newer.
- `init.go:187` — `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "init: refreshed %s ...")` fires
  on every overwrite of an existing file, whether the overwrite is a genuine
  upgrade or (today) a silent rollback; there is no separate "downgraded"
  path.

Verdict: **LIVE**, and the ticket's own repro (fixture rev `8331a94`
2026-06-05 vs `089cd31` 2026-06-29, Context section) is consistent with what
the code does: `isCleanUpgrade` returns true on hash equality alone, so the
older binary's embedded copy overwrites the newer on-disk asset, and
`assetDriftWarnings`/`init.go:187` narrate it as an upgrade throughout.

## Plan

### Actions

1. **`manifest.go`, new manifest-date reader (sibling accessor, per the
   Imagine finding — `parseManifest` today only extracts `" sha256:"` lines
   and silently drops `rev:`/`date:`).** Add, near `parseManifest`
   (manifest.go:105-126):
   ```go
   // parseManifestDate extracts the "date: " header line from manifest bytes,
   // mirroring parseManifest's line-scan style. Returns "" when absent —
   // including a manifest from an erg predating the date: field.
   func parseManifestDate(data []byte) string {
       const prefix = "date:"
       for _, line := range strings.Split(string(data), "\n") {
           line = strings.TrimSpace(line)
           if strings.HasPrefix(line, prefix) {
               return strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimPrefix(line, prefix))
           }
       }
       return ""
   }

   // manifestDateFile reads the date: field from the manifest at path ("" if
   // absent/unreadable). Sibling of readManifestFile, same absence contract.
   func manifestDateFile(path string) string {
       data, err := os.ReadFile(path)
       if err != nil {
           return ""
       }
       return parseManifestDate(data)
   }

   // manifestDate reads root's tickets/.erg-assets date: field. Sibling of
   // readManifest.
   func manifestDate(root string) string {
       return manifestDateFile(filepath.Join(root, "tickets", manifestName))
   }
   ```
   This is additive — `parseManifest`, `readManifest`, `readManifestFile` and
   their existing callers/tests (manifest_test.go `TestReadManifest`) are
   untouched. `buildManifest` (manifest.go:57-59) writes the literal string
   `"unknown"` when `buildDate` is empty, so `manifestDate` can return
   `"unknown"` — treat that as "no date" everywhere below (Action 5).

2. **`manifest.go`, new direction predicate.** Add next to `isCleanUpgrade`:
   ```go
   // isRollback reports whether stampDate is strictly newer than
   // runningDate — the running binary predates the last init. Plain lexical
   // comparison on the ISO-8601 buildDate format (version.go:237 precedent,
   // Makefile:35 `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`) — no time.Parse. Either side
   // missing or "unknown" means no provenance to compare, so it is NOT a
   // rollback: falls through to today's behaviour (ticket's dateless-stamp
   // invariant).
   func isRollback(stampDate, runningDate string) bool {
       if stampDate == "" || stampDate == "unknown" || runningDate == "" {
           return false
       }
       return stampDate > runningDate
   }
   ```

3. **`manifest.go:163-173`, `isCleanUpgrade` signature/logic change.** Add
   two string params and consult `isRollback` only on the stamp-match branch
   (history-hash branch is untouched — it already requires exact equality to
   a *known shipped* hash, so a rollback there would need a `known` entry
   from a genuinely newer release, out of scope for this stamp-based fix):
   ```go
   func isCleanUpgrade(diskHash, stampedHash string, known []string, stampDate, runningDate string) bool {
       if stampedHash != "" {
           if diskHash != stampedHash {
               return false
           }
           return !isRollback(stampDate, runningDate)
       }
       for _, h := range known {
           if diskHash == h {
               return true
           }
       }
       return false
   }
   ```
   Call site `init.go:150`:
   ```go
   if !isCleanUpgrade(diskHash, stamps[name], knownAssetHashes(rel), manifestDate(root), buildDate) {
       preserve = true
   }
   ```
   This changes `isCleanUpgrade`'s signature, so every existing call in
   `manifest_test.go` `TestIsCleanUpgrade` (6 cases, lines 29-36) needs the
   two new trailing args — pass `("", "")` for all of them (no date recorded
   ⇒ behaviour must be byte-identical to today, which is itself a regression
   assertion worth keeping explicit rather than silently satisfied).

4. **`manifest.go:24`, `assetDriftSignal` direction split.** Keep the
   existing constant (its exact string is `update.go:228`'s grep target —
   do not change it) and add a sibling:
   ```go
   const assetDriftSignal = "differs from the .erg-assets stamp (binary upgraded since last init) -- run 'erg init' to refresh"
   const assetRollbackSignal = "differs from the .erg-assets stamp (this binary predates the last init) -- run 'erg update' first, then 'erg init'"
   ```
   `assetDriftWarnings` (manifest.go:184-208): compute `stampDate :=
   manifestDateFile(filepath.Join(dir, manifestName))` once, then per asset:
   ```go
   if stamp != sha256hex([]byte(content)) {
       signal := assetDriftSignal
       if isRollback(stampDate, buildDate) {
           signal = assetRollbackSignal
       }
       warnings = append(warnings, fmt.Sprintf("WARN %s: embedded version %s", name, signal))
   }
   ```
   (The `-- run 'erg init' to refresh` suffix moves inside each constant so
   the rollback message can name its own remedy — Action 3 of the ticket's
   own Actions section.)

5. **`init.go:160`, the preserve-time stderr message.** Today it
   unconditionally says `"has local edits -- preserving (run with --force to
   overwrite)"`, which is false for a rollback (nothing was locally edited).
   Branch it on the same `isRollback` call used in step 3's `preserve`
   decision — reuse the boolean rather than recomputing:
   ```go
   if preserve {
       skipped++
       msg := "has local edits -- preserving (run with --force to overwrite)"
       if isRollback(stampDate, buildDate) {
           msg = "is newer than this binary -- preserving (run 'erg update' first, then 'erg init' --force to override)"
       }
       ...
       fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "init: %s %s\n", rel, msg)
       continue
   }
   ```
   Requires hoisting `stampDate := manifestDate(root)` once near
   `installAssets`'s existing `stamps := readManifest(root)` (init.go:117),
   not per-asset.

6. **`init.go:165-188`, `installAssets` "downgraded" vs "refreshed" label
   (ticket Action 4, the `--force` path).** When `refuseDiverged` is false
   (`erg init --force`, `erg migrate`), `preserve` is never set, so a
   rollback silently overwrites today. Label the outcome using the same
   `isRollback(stampDate, buildDate)`:
   ```go
   if exists {
       refreshed++
   } else {
       created++
   }
   verb, pastVerb := "would refresh", "refreshed"
   if exists && isRollback(stampDate, buildDate) {
       verb, pastVerb = "would downgrade", "downgraded"
   }
   if dryRun {
       v := "would create "
       if exists {
           v = verb
       }
       fmt.Printf("  %s  %s\n", v, rel)
       continue
   }
   ...
   if exists {
       fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "init: %s %s (git restore -- %s to undo)\n", pastVerb, rel, rel)
   }
   ```
   `migrateAssetPaths`'s caller (`erg migrate`) is unaffected in practice —
   `AGENTS.md` there is force-overwritten unconditionally by design
   (init.go:16-19 comment), and if its stamp is ever a rollback the label
   now correctly says `downgraded` instead of misreporting `refreshed`; no
   behaviour change, only the printed verb.
   `stampDate` here is the *one* value read at the top of `installAssets`
   (step 5), reused for both the preserve-message branch and this label —
   not recomputed per asset.

7. **Dateless-stamp fallback (ticket Action 5).** No dedicated code path
   needed — it falls out of `isRollback`'s guard clause (`stampDate == "" ||
   stampDate == "unknown"`) added in step 2. Call out explicitly in review:
   a manifest from a pre-`date:` erg, or one whose `date:` line is absent for
   any other reason, makes `manifestDate`/`manifestDateFile` return `""`,
   which routes every call site back to the pre-fix behaviour.

### First test (red step)

All new/changed tests live in `src/go/manifest_test.go` (existing convention
— `installAssets`/`isCleanUpgrade` are tested there; there is no
`init_test.go` and none should be added for this).

Add a new test function, e.g. `TestInstallAssetsRollbackPreserved`, built the
same way as the existing `TestInstallAssetsHistoricalUpgrade` /
`TestInstallAssetsUnknownPreserved` (t.TempDir, write a `.erg-assets`
manifest via `writeManifestFile`, write a matching on-disk asset, call
`installAssets` directly), covering four sub-cases as `t.Run` arms:

- **`t.Run("newer stamp -> preserved", ...)` — THE RED STEP.** Fixture:
  `.erg-assets` with `date: 2099-01-01T00:00:00Z` (or any date lexically
  greater than the test binary's `buildDate`, which in `go test` is `""` —
  so set `buildDate = "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"` for the test, `t.Cleanup` to
  restore `""`) and an asset hash matching the stamped hash exactly. Call
  `installAssets(root, initAssetPaths, true, false)` and assert `skipped ==
  1` and the on-disk content is **unchanged** (still the "old" content, not
  overwritten with the embedded one). **Against today's code this fails**:
  `isCleanUpgrade` ignores date entirely, sees `diskHash == stampedHash`,
  returns `true`, `installAssets` overwrites and reports `refreshed == 1`,
  `skipped == 0` — run this test before touching `manifest.go`/`init.go` and
  confirm the failure, per the ticket's own instruction ("run it and watch
  it fail before the fix, or it proves nothing"). This is the literal
  `installAssets`-level proxy for `cmdInit`'s `skipped > 0 => return 2`
  (init.go:251-253) — `cmdInit` itself is not separately tested anywhere in
  the codebase today (grep confirms no `init_test.go`, no exec-the-binary
  exit-code test exists for `init`), so testing at the `installAssets`
  return-value level matches existing convention rather than introducing a
  new test shape.
- **`t.Run("older stamp -> upgraded", ...)` — the positive control (see
  Antipattern scan below).** Same fixture shape, `date:` lexically *older*
  than `buildDate` (e.g. stamp `"2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"`, binary
  `"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"`), disk asset matching the stamped hash. Assert
  `refreshed == 1`, `skipped == 0`, on-disk content becomes the embedded
  content. Must pass both before and after the fix.
- **`t.Run("dateless stamp -> unchanged behaviour", ...)`.** Manifest with no
  `date:` line at all (or a manifest written by the pre-existing
  `writeManifestFile` helper with only `assets:` lines). Assert the outcome
  is identical to `TestInstallAssetsHistoricalUpgrade`'s existing case
  (clean upgrade proceeds) — this is a straight regression guard, not new
  behaviour.
- **`t.Run("rollback message names the remedy, not upgraded", ...)`** — this
  one asserts the *text*, not counts, so it needs `installAssets` (or, more
  directly, `assetDriftWarnings`) to be driven with stderr captured, or
  simpler: add a narrow `TestAssetDriftWarningsRollbackDirection` calling
  `assetDriftWarnings(dir)` directly on a fixture with a newer stamp than
  `buildDate` and asserting the returned warning string contains
  `"erg update"` and does **not** contain the substring `"upgraded"`. This
  is the ticket's fourth arm ("message rather than exit code") and is
  invisible to any exit-code-only assertion — keep it as its own test, not
  folded into the counts-based `installAssets` cases.

A fifth, smaller test worth adding directly in the same file:
`TestIsRollback` (pure-function table test, same style as
`TestIsCleanUpgrade`) covering: newer/older/equal dates, empty stamp date,
empty running date, and `stampDate == "unknown"` — this is the cheapest
place to lock in the lexical-compare contract before wiring it into
`isCleanUpgrade`/`assetDriftWarnings`/`installAssets`.

### Dependencies

None — this is wave 1. The fix is confined to `manifest.go` + `init.go` (one
new call-site argument in each of `installAssets` and `assetDriftWarnings`)
and does not touch `update.go` beyond relying on `assetDriftSignal`'s string
value staying fixed (confirmed unchanged in Action 4 above). No ordering
constraint against 0255, 0256, 0278 in this raid wave.

### Antipattern scan — tautological tests

- **"newer stamp -> preserved" (the red step):** catches a WRONG
  implementation (today's direction-blind `isCleanUpgrade`) — confirmed
  failing pre-fix by construction (traced above). It would **not** by itself
  catch an implementation that refuses *all* upgrades regardless of
  direction (e.g. `isCleanUpgrade` returns `false` whenever both a stamp
  date and a running date are present) — that broken implementation would
  make this test pass for the wrong reason.
- **"older stamp -> upgraded" (the positive control) is therefore
  load-bearing, exactly as the ticket says**, and it is present in the plan
  above as its own `t.Run` arm. Verified it would actually fail against an
  inverted or over-eager direction check: if someone flips `isRollback`'s
  comparison operator (`<` instead of `>`) or drops the guard and refuses on
  *any* date mismatch, the older-stamp case now (wrongly) sets `preserve =
  true`, so `skipped` becomes `1` instead of `0` and the assertion
  `skipped == 0` fails. Confirmed load-bearing, not decorative.
- **"dateless stamp -> unchanged"** catches a DIFFERENT-implementation risk
  more than a wrong one on its own (an implementation that treats
  `stampDate == ""` as "very old" or "very new" instead of "unknown" would
  fail it, which is the correct thing for it to catch) — kept as a named
  arm because Action 5 is explicit ticket-level scope, not because it is a
  strong discriminator by itself.
- **"message names the remedy, not upgraded"** catches a WRONG
  implementation that gets the exit code right (arm 1 passes) but leaves
  `assetDriftSignal`'s text unchanged — this is precisely defect 1 from the
  ticket's Context (the warning asserting a direction it never checked), and
  no count-based assertion anywhere else in this plan would catch a
  regression here. Necessary, not redundant with arm 1.
- **`TestIsRollback`** is a pure-function unit test of a two-line
  comparison; by itself it is nearly tautological (it will pass for any
  implementation that literally does `stampDate > runningDate` with the
  stated guards) but it is not redundant: it isolates the lexical-compare
  contract from the three call sites that consume it, so a future edit to
  `isRollback` alone gets a fast, precise failure instead of only a
  downstream symptom in `installAssets`.

### Exit-code contract check

`grep -n "return 2" src/go/*.go` finds exactly two occurrences, both in
`init.go` (`cmdInit`, lines 245 and 252), both gated on `skipped > 0` from
`installAssets`'s return values, and both documented identically: "2 local
edits were preserved and skipped" (init.go:88-90, and `helpInit`'s
user-facing text). No other command in the codebase returns 2 (`check.go`
explicitly documents "check never returns 2"; `validate.go` only returns 0/1).

**Exit 2 is the right code for the rollback-preserve case, and no new code
is needed to wire it** — this plan's step 3 makes `isCleanUpgrade` return
`false` for a rollback, which routes through the *existing* `preserve = true`
→ `skipped++` → `cmdInit`'s `skipped > 0 => return 2` path unchanged. The
only correctness question is semantic, not mechanical: exit 2 is currently
documented as "local edits were preserved," and a rollback-preserve is not a
local edit. Recommend leaving the exit code at 2 (it is already the
"something needs your attention before this can proceed automatically, rerun
with --force to override" code, which is exactly the rollback case too) but
tightening `helpInit`'s exit-code prose (init.go:88-90) to cover both reasons
a file can be preserved, e.g. "2 a file was preserved because it either has
local edits or is newer than this binary (rerun with --force to override)" —
a doc-only change, not a new exit code, and in scope for this PR since the
ticket's exit criteria list "No message asserts an ordering the code has not
established" and the current doc string implicitly does (it names only the
local-edit reason).
